The gspca core uses alt_xfer which loops over bNumEndpoints, so it's safe. The gspca subdrivers (stv06xx, xirlink_cit, konica, ov519, pb0100) in this snapshot s12 all have the bNumEndpoints checks and num_altsetting checks — they appear FIXED in this tree. flexcop-usb also has the checks but the check at 512 is `bNumEndpoints < 1` yet reads endpoint[1] — an off-by-one bug! Let me examine this closely.

flexcop-usb.c:
- Line 511-514:
```c
	if (fc_usb->uintf->cur_altsetting->desc.bNumEndpoints < 1)
		return -ENODEV;
	if (!usb_endpoint_is_isoc_in(&fc_usb->uintf->cur_altsetting->endpoint[1].desc))
		return -ENODEV;
```

This checks bNumEndpoints < 1 (i.e., at least 1 endpoint) but then indexes endpoint[1]! If bNumEndpoints == 1, the check passes (1 < 1 is false) but endpoint[1] is out of bounds. This is a NEW variant: an off-by-one bounds check. Interesting! This is in flexcop_usb_init, called from probe after usb_set_interface(udev, 0, 1).

Wait — let me be careful. Is the check `bNumEndpoints < 1` present in upstream Linux? Upstream commit 7edaf3b7... Let me recall: there was a fix for flexcop-usb "b2c2: Add missing usb endpoint check" — the upstream code is:

```c
	if (fc_usb->uintf->cur_altsetting->desc.bNumEndpoints < 1)
		return -ENODEV;
	if (!usb_endpoint_is_isoc_in(&fc_usb->uintf->cur_altsetting->endpoint[1].desc))
		return -ENODEV;
```

Hmm, actually that IS what upstream has. This is a known off-by-one: bNumEndpoints < 1 means >= 1 endpoint (index 0 valid), but endpoint[1] requires >= 2. So with bNumEndpoints == 1, endpoint[1] reads OOB. Actually wait — I recall the actual upstream fix commit: "media: usb: b2c2: fix an off-by-one bounds check" or similar? Let me check git history knowledge. Actually there's CVE-2022-... hmm. I recall commit "b2c2: flexcop-usb: fix ..." Not sure. Regardless, in this snapshot the code as-is reads endpoint[1] after only checking bNumEndpoints >= 1. That is exactly the seed mechanism: bNumEndpoints declared by device = 1, driver assumes index 1 exists (needs 2 endpoints). OOB read.

Also endpoint[0].desc.wMaxPacketSize in transfer_init at line 427-429: is there any check on bNumEndpoints >= 1 before transfer_init? flexcop_usb_init checks bNumEndpoints < 1 AFTER calling usb_set_interface(udev, 0, 1) — and flexcop_usb_transfer_init is called after flexcop_usb_init in probe. So endpoint[0] access at line 429 is guarded by the bNumEndpoints < 1 check in flexcop_usb_init (which runs first and returns -ENODEV before transfer_init). Wait — order in probe: flexcop_usb_init(fc_usb) → checks. Then flexcop_device_initialize. Then flexcop_usb_transfer_init. So endpoint[0] read at 429 is preceded by bNumEndpoints >= 1 check. Good — endpoint[0] access is validated (index 0 needs bNumEndpoints >= 1, and the check < 1 rejects 0). So endpoint[0] is fine.

But endpoint[1] at line 514 with check `bNumEndpoints < 1` is off-by-one: requires bNumEndpoints >= 2 for endpoint[1], but check only enforces >= 1. This is a genuine finding of the seed mechanism class: "missing/insufficient bounds validation of the endpoint array before indexing". The invariant: code must validate bNumEndpoints > k before indexing endpoint[k]. Here k=1 but the check validates only k=0's requirement.

So finding #1: drivers/media/usb/b2c2/flexcop-usb.c:511-514, off-by-one bounds check, endpoint[1] OOB read at probe (usb_set_interface to alt 1 then cur_altsetting read) when malicious FlexCop-spoofing device declares bNumEndpoints=1 in altsetting 1 (or the current altsetting). Note: the device must match USB_DEVICE(0x0af7, 0x0101) id — attacker-controlled gadget can present that VID/PID. The read is `usb_endpoint_is_isoc_in(&...endpoint[1].desc)` — an OOB read of the endpoint array (the endpoint array is a flex array in usb_host_interface, sized bNumEndpoints; allocated in usb core config parsing with exactly bNumEndpoints entries). OOB read of descriptor memory; value used to decide ENODEV. Consequence: OOB read at probe — could read past the allocated interface cache. Severity is mainly DoS/info-less read. Still fits the mechanism.

